1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,703
Two 8 gigabyte sticks of RAM can be faster than one 16 gigabyte stick.

2
00:00:05,983 --> 00:00:07,802
Same total memory, so why?

3
00:00:08,552 --> 00:00:12,937
Your processor talks to memory through channels, like lanes on a road.

4
00:00:13,217 --> 00:00:15,681
Most desktop processors have two of them.

5
00:00:16,431 --> 00:00:19,450
With a single stick, only one lane is in use.

6
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:25,583
Install two matching sticks in the right slots, and both lanes carry data at the same time.

7
00:00:26,333 --> 00:00:30,690
That roughly doubles the peak bandwidth between the processor and memory.

8
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:34,424
Games and built-in graphics often feel the difference most.

9
00:00:35,174 --> 00:00:38,193
Check your motherboard manual for which slots to use.

10
00:00:38,473 --> 00:00:40,257
It's often the second and fourth.

11
00:00:40,537 --> 00:00:43,103
More PC hardware explained on the channel.

